Handle the IPv4 peers of a dual-stack [::] socket as IPv4.

Kamailio never sets IPV6_V6ONLY, so a socket on [::] also takes IPv4, but
the core had no idea of it: an IPv4 peer arrived as ::ffff:a.b.c.d with af
INET6, and nothing could be sent to an IPv4 address through that socket
(get_send_socket() refuses a forced socket of the other family, and the
first IPv4 socket is whatever else happens to listen on IPv4). Which is why
a node that serves both families listens twice, on 0.0.0.0 and on [::],
and the TCP pair only binds side by side with tcp_reuse_port.

A [::] socket the kernel lets take IPv4 (IPV6_V6ONLY off, which is the
default of net.ipv6.bindv6only) is marked SI_IS_DUALSTACK at startup, and:

- an IPv4 peer it receives from, over UDP or accepted over TCP, is turned
  back into the IPv4 address it is, with the IPv4 any as the local one, so
  $si, $af, Via received, usrloc and the rest see an IPv4 message, as on a
  0.0.0.0 socket;
- udp_send() maps an IPv4 destination to ::ffff:a.b.c.d on it;
- it serves IPv4 for get_send_socket(): a forced send socket, the socket
  the request came in on, and sendipv4/sendipv4_tcp, the first socket that
  takes IPv4 in listen order;
- where a socket is looked up by the local IPv4 address the kernel picked
  (mhomed get_out_socket(), the source of a new TCP connection) and none is
  bound to that address, the dual-stack socket takes it, as a 0.0.0.0 one
  would, rather than the first IPv4 socket bound to some other address;
- a new TCP connection to an IPv4 server sourced from it binds the IPv4 any
  with the same port instead of failing to bind [::].

A socket bound to a specific IPv6 address, or one with IPV6_V6ONLY on, is
left as it was.

